<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Harsha</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 590–647</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Also known as Harshavardhana</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Harsha, or Harshavardhana, was the most famous ruler of the Pushyabhuti dynasty, reigning from 606 to 647 from Thanesar and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannauj">Kannauj</a> over much of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. A patron of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> and letters, he wrote three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> plays and was described by the Chinese pilgrim <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xuanzang">Xuanzang</a> and his court poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banabhatta">Bana</a>. His southern advance was stopped by the Chalukya king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pulakeshin-ii">Pulakeshin II</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/harsha.jpg" alt="Harsha" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CNG Coins · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Harshavardhana_Circa_AD_606-647.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
